.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_DYNQ 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_dynq \- build a ccol_selectable from a ccol_dynamic_queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable_from_dynq(q, dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Constructs a ccol_selectable tagged-union value of type ccol_selectable_dynq
wrapping the given ccol_dynamic_queue pointer with the specified direction.

  ccol_select_read; wait for at least one message to arrive (read-wait)
  ccol_select_write; wait until sending is enabled and the queue holds
                     fewer than ccol_max_elem_count messages, the same
                     ceiling ccol_dynmq_send_zc() itself enforces

Because a ccol_dynamic_queue is unbounded in practice, it is always considered
writable as long as writing is not disabled.  ccol_select_write on a
ccol_dynamic_queue is only necessary when ccol_dynmq_disable_sending() has been
called and the caller needs to know when the queue becomes writable again.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
q    ccol_dynamic_queue pointer to wrap.  Passing NULL produces a selectable
     that ccol_select() will reject with ccol_invalid_args.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_selectable value (not a pointer).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_dynamic_queue *dq = ccol_dynamic_queue_create(NULL);
ccol_circular_queue *q0 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);

size_t idx;
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_dynq(dq, ccol_select_read));
/* on ccol_success, claim the message via ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(dq, ...) or
   ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(q0, ...), whichever selectable idx points at */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_select (3),
.BR ccol_select_va (3),
.BR ccol_selectable_from_circq (3),
.BR ccol_selectable_from_chan (3),
.BR ccol_selectable_from_fd (3)
